WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.489 --> 00:00:03.129
This video was made possible by CuriosityStream.

00:00:03.129 --> 00:00:07.709
Sign up for the CuriosityStream/Nebula bundle
deal at CuriosityStream.com/Wendover to watch

00:00:07.709 --> 00:00:12.190
an exclusive companion video diving deeper
into this video’s subject.

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:16.720
The first thing to know about the art market
is that it’s tiny.

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.240
Despite its prominence in cinema, literature,
and the news, the majority of the market is

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:25.820
composed of a small collection of dealers
and auctioneers catering to an exclusive list

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:27.830
of ultra-rich clientele.

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:33.500
Each year, on average, $61.9 billion of art
is sold.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:38.800
$61.9 billion is a big number, but not in
context.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:44.440
$61.9 billion is what Walmart, for example,
sells in just 41 days.

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:51.000
The global market of tomatoes generates $190
billion in annual sales—three times as much

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.000
as art.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.480
There are single companies with greater annual
sales that you probably haven’t even heard

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:02.530
of: AXA, the Centene Corporation, Exor, the
McKesson Corporation—the entire art market

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:04.101
is smaller than each of these.

00:01:04.101 --> 00:01:10.670
The second thing to know about the art market
is that it’s extraordinarily consolidated.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:17.689
Over the past decade, 82% of sales, by value,
were made in the US, UK, or greater China.

00:01:17.689 --> 00:01:23.689
That’s to say, the global art market is
a phenomenon largely restricted to New York,

00:01:23.689 --> 00:01:25.079
London, and Hong Kong.

00:01:25.079 --> 00:01:29.229
But the consolidation doesn’t end there.

00:01:29.229 --> 00:01:35.329
43% of art dealers, nearly half, had fewer
than 20 unique buyers in 2020.

00:01:35.329 --> 00:01:40.369
Their sale and buyer numbers can be as limited
as those of realtors, which starts to make

00:01:40.369 --> 00:01:44.429
sense when comparing the sale prices of the
two markets.

00:01:44.429 --> 00:01:50.439
Unsurprisingly, this means that power in the
industry is extraordinarily concentrated too.

00:01:50.439 --> 00:01:55.840
One study found that 30% of solo exhibitions
at museums in the US, considered the hallmark

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:00.829
of success, featured artists represented by
one of just five galleries.

00:02:00.829 --> 00:02:06.479
Once you move into the upper echelons of the
industry, however, the concentration intensifies.

00:02:06.479 --> 00:02:12.720
During the studied period, eleven out of twelve
solo exhibitions at the Guggenheim, considered

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:17.690
one of the world’s preeminent art museums,
presented artists represented by one of the

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:19.720
same five galleries.

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:27.420
So, to summarize, the key features of this
market are that it is small and concentrated—without

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:32.280
knowing anything more, an economist could
tell you that this is, theoretically, a market

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:35.110
ripe for exploitation and corruption.

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:37.770
But that’s just theory.

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:44.270
To understand where the malpractice occurs,
one must first understand the practice—how

00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:45.980
art sales work.

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:52.260
Nearly three decades ago, in 1994, artist
Roy Lichtenstein put on one of the last solo

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:54.300
shows of his career.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:03:00.000
For this, he created a series of nude portraits—a
novel subject for Lichtenstein, presented

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.660
in his archetypical style.

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:07.100
Now, in order to translate his work into income,
Lichtenstein partnered with the Leo Castelli

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:08.100
Gallery.

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:12.820
This was no surprise, as Castelli and his
gallery had been long-time partners of the

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:14.450
pop-art icon.

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:19.590
Three decades prior, Castelli had presented
Lichtenstein’s first-ever one-man show.

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:23.690
The two had formed a professional relationship
over the years prior while Lichtenstein worked

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:28.800
as an assistant art professor at Rutgers University
and Castelli believed his work could be a

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:30.720
commercial success.

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:37.280
He was, of course, correct, as every single
piece was sold prior to the show’s opening.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:40.060
This skyrocketed Lichtenstein’s career.

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:45.370
Overnight, he was transformed from, essentially,
a hobbyist, into an internationally-known,

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:49.410
commercially-successful, professional artist.

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:55.060
So, for all intents and purposes, Castelli
made Lichtenstein.

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:59.460
Therefore, Lichtenstein stuck with Castelli
throughout his career, which brought him to

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:04.360
that 1994 exhibition—entitled “Nudes:
9 Color Relief Prints.”

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:09.260
As is common in the high-end art world, it’s
difficult to precisely trace the ownership

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:14.100
history of given works, but we know that one
of the pieces, Nude with Joyous Painting,

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:18.609
was sold by the Castelli Gallery to a private
collector in New York, then sold by the James

00:04:18.609 --> 00:04:23.090
Goodman Gallery to a private collector in
Detroit, before it was purchased by an unknown,

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:24.090
new owner.

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:28.060
That new owner then decided, in 2020, to sell
the painting.

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:30.340
Therefore, they contacted Christie’s.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:36.159
Now, Christie’s and it’s primary competitor,
Sotheby’s, are the big movers and shakers

00:04:36.159 --> 00:04:37.409
in the art market.

00:04:37.409 --> 00:04:42.180
They’re auction houses, and facilitate the
sale of much of the world’s most expensive

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:43.330
art.

00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:50.050
Of the ten highest priced sales in 2020, eight
occurred at either Christie’s or Sotheby’s.

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:52.330
Their process starts with an appraisal.

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:56.830
Each auction house employs in-house experts
who determine what they believe a piece will

00:04:56.830 --> 00:05:01.080
sell for, and present a range—a lower number
and a higher number.

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:04.990
Then, assuming they want to move forward,
the auction house and seller will mutually

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:10.240
agree to a reserve price—a number equal
to or less than the lower range of the estimate,

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:14.139
under which they will not sell the piece,
even if an offer is submitted.

00:05:14.139 --> 00:05:17.830
Typically, with higher-end sales, however,
the auction house will persuade the seller

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:22.729
to use their services by guaranteeing the
sale—agreeing to purchase the artwork themselves

00:05:22.729 --> 00:05:26.330
for the lower end of the estimate, even if
no bids are submitted.

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:30.950
On the other end of the spectrum, the auction
house takes a 2% fee if a piece of artwork

00:05:30.950 --> 00:05:35.639
is sold above the upper estimate to incentivize
performance, although this is often waived

00:05:35.639 --> 00:05:37.780
in contract negotiations with the seller.

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:42.999
The seller also pays a percentage fee, supposedly
as much as 10% for lower-priced works, and

00:05:42.999 --> 00:05:47.449
as low as 2% for higher-priced works, although
official numbers are not published and are

00:05:47.449 --> 00:05:52.400
highly subject to negotiation as auction houses
are eager to bring in sellers.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:55.740
The bulk of the auction house’s money is
made from the buyer.

00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:59.330
They pay, on top of the final sale price,
a percentage fee.

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:05.229
At most Christie’s auction houses, it’s
25% on sales up to $600,000, 20% for sales

00:06:05.229 --> 00:06:09.870
up to $6,000,000, then 14.5% on sales above
that number.

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:14.479
This is all to say, when Christie’s or Sotheby’s
gets their hands on the kind of artwork that

00:06:14.479 --> 00:06:20.279
lists for 10s of millions of dollars, they
are highly, highly financially motivated to

00:06:20.279 --> 00:06:21.949
make the sale.

00:06:21.949 --> 00:06:26.409
Roy Lichtenstein’s Nude with Joyous Painting
falls into this category.

00:06:26.409 --> 00:06:31.130
A deceased, highly commercially successful
artist; a break-from-style piece, created

00:06:31.130 --> 00:06:35.960
towards the end of its artist’s career—there
was a lot going for this painting, and Christie’s

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:37.199
knew it.

00:06:37.199 --> 00:06:42.870
Therefore, they scheduled its auction to take
place at one of their top events of the year.

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:47.749
Under the constraints of the COVID-19 pandemic,
the auction house organized a new format for

00:06:47.749 --> 00:06:50.150
one of 2020’s biggest auctions.

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:54.819
While each of their auctions typically occur
under the banner of one of their locations,

00:06:54.819 --> 00:07:00.219
their Hong Kong, Paris, London, and New York
auction houses teamed up to run a simultaneous,

00:07:00.219 --> 00:07:01.889
global, live-streamed auction.

00:07:01.889 --> 00:07:08.280
Now, of the 79 lots put up for auction, the
absolute least expensive was a piece by Matthew

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:12.520
Wong appraised between $60,000 and $80,000.

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:16.650
Most were well into the millions, and this
was by design.

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:20.699
Christie’s, Sotheby’s, and other auction
houses will aggregate their highest-priced

00:07:20.699 --> 00:07:25.509
works together into a few yearly auctions,
so that they can turn them into can’t miss

00:07:25.509 --> 00:07:27.620
events for the art world.

00:07:27.620 --> 00:07:31.999
Their job is essentially the marketing—they’re
trying to build anticipation and excitement

00:07:31.999 --> 00:07:35.910
for the sale of a given piece—so they’ll
spend the months prior working their catalogue

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:41.039
of buyers, talking to the press, and exhibiting
works all around the world.

00:07:41.039 --> 00:07:47.610
Then, after almost all of the work is completed,
the night of the auction arrives.

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:50.759
In this case, the event started with Hong
Kong.

00:07:50.759 --> 00:07:56.050
Their auctioneer, live-streamed to the world,
sold nine relatively lower-priced works before

00:07:56.050 --> 00:08:00.900
passing the torch to Paris, from where 15
higher priced lots were auctioned.

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:05.669
Then, it moved onto London, where hammer prices
crept further and further into the millions,

00:08:05.669 --> 00:08:08.240
and finally, it was New York’s turn.

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:13.560
Saving the best for last, all of the true
headliners were auctioned from New York.

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:19.490
This included Picasso’s, Basquiat’s, Calder’s,
O’Keeffe’s, Warhol’s, and, of course,

00:08:19.490 --> 00:08:20.830
the Lichtenstein.

00:08:20.830 --> 00:08:25.330
Bidding opened on Nude with Joyous Painting
at $20 million.

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:31.680
That was quickly pushed up to 22, 26, 28,
then 29 million dollars.

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:35.949
Two anonymous bidders from New York, aided
by phone-laden representatives at the auction

00:08:35.949 --> 00:08:40.709
house, pushed the price up by half-million
increments to 36, until a buyer from Hong

00:08:40.709 --> 00:08:43.919
Kong swooped in and bid $37 million.

00:08:43.919 --> 00:08:47.960
One of the New York bidders responded with
$38 million, then the other New York bidder

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:49.620
came back with 38.2.

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:59.709
It went to 38.5, 39, 39.5, 40, before the
Hong Kong buyer bid $40.5 million, which met

00:08:59.709 --> 00:09:05.089
no response, and the auctioneer’s hammer
came down on what would become the third highest-priced

00:09:05.089 --> 00:09:07.140
art sale of the year.

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:16.699
With fees, the buyer ended up paying $46,242,500,
meaning Christie’s netted at least $5,742,500

00:09:16.699 --> 00:09:22.130
from the 10 minute and 14 second long auction.

00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:27.990
Across the evening, though, some $421 million
of art was sold.

00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:34.459
That means this one, four-hour event, and
its 79 lots, accounted for almost a full percent

00:09:34.459 --> 00:09:40.329
of the entire world’s art sales in 2020,
by value, and that Christie’s, as a company,

00:09:40.329 --> 00:09:43.990
earned upwards of $60 million.

00:09:43.990 --> 00:09:47.759
So… where’s the problem?

00:09:47.759 --> 00:09:52.560
So far, most of this process probably seems
above-board—its just a collection of wealthy

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:57.240
people buying and selling expensive art through
a few exclusive auction houses.

00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:02.410
Well, its not so much the process itself,
but what it enables.

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:08.519
Most of the issues arise out of a simple truth—art
does not have intrinsic value.

00:10:08.519 --> 00:10:14.160
Say you stopped by a thrift store, saw this
painting on the wall, and it had no label,

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:19.120
no price tag, no information at all beyond
the piece itself.

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:21.100
How much would you pay for it?

00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:26.210
Some would only spare twenty or thirty dollars,
while others, impressed by its intricacy and

00:10:26.210 --> 00:10:29.350
age, might pay a couple hundred dollars for
it.

00:10:29.350 --> 00:10:34.670
This is roughly what happened in 1958 when
it was presented at a small auction in London

00:10:34.670 --> 00:10:40.170
as a poorly-maintained copy of a lost work
by Leonardo Da Vinci, and sold for £45 to

00:10:40.170 --> 00:10:42.149
Minnie and Warren Kuntz.

00:10:42.149 --> 00:10:45.630
The couple carried the painting with them
to Southampton, then boarded a ship bound

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:48.839
for Houston to return from their European
vacation.

00:10:48.839 --> 00:10:54.310
In 1987, the artwork was inherited by Kuntz’s
nephew, Basil Clovis Hendry, who hung it in

00:10:54.310 --> 00:10:58.120
his Baton Rouge, Louisiana home until his
death in 2004.

00:10:58.120 --> 00:11:03.230
His daughter, Susan Hendry Tureau, brought
the work and much of the rest of her father’s

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:07.730
estate to the New Orleans Auction Gallery,
where it was listed with a sale estimate of

00:11:07.730 --> 00:11:10.310
$1,200 to $1,800.

00:11:10.310 --> 00:11:15.160
It was bought for an unknown amount less than
$10,000 by a pair of art dealers, Alexander

00:11:15.160 --> 00:11:20.839
Parrish and Robert Simon, who had a long-shot
hunch that it could be worth far more than

00:11:20.839 --> 00:11:21.839
that.

00:11:21.839 --> 00:11:25.910
They therefore hired a paintings conservator
from NYU to restore the work, and as part

00:11:25.910 --> 00:11:30.279
of her process, she took infrared photographs
of the piece to be able to see through the

00:11:30.279 --> 00:11:31.920
layers of paint.

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:37.120
This unearthed the lowest layer, which included
the very first strokes—the ones the artist

00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:40.589
took to outline the general composition of
the painting.

00:11:40.589 --> 00:11:46.430
These first strokes largely matched up with
the final product, except for here—the thumb.

00:11:46.430 --> 00:11:52.140
They outlined the thumb in a straight position,
rather than the curved shape seen in all known

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:54.899
copies of the lost Da Vinci painting.

00:11:54.899 --> 00:12:02.230
It seemed that the artist changed their mind,
which would make no sense when copying a painting.

00:12:02.230 --> 00:12:08.149
These few, hidden strokes served as a crucial,
initial piece of evidence that this might

00:12:08.149 --> 00:12:13.680
be the original Salvator Mundi, painted by
Leonardo Da Vinci himself.

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:18.990
Therefore, Parrish and Simon spent the better
part of the next five years travelling around,

00:12:18.990 --> 00:12:23.449
showing the piece to experts and curators,
garnering support for their theory.

00:12:23.449 --> 00:12:29.680
In 2011, they attained definitive validation:
London’s National Gallery exhibited the

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:34.529
Salvator Mundi and the name on the label read
Leonardo Da Vinci.

00:12:34.529 --> 00:12:37.490
The value instantly skyrocketed.

00:12:37.490 --> 00:12:42.209
Parrish and Simon sold the piece to another
dealer for $80 million, who immediately sold

00:12:42.209 --> 00:12:48.480
it to a Russian collector for $127 million,
who then brought the piece to auction.

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:53.540
Only the 24th known painting widely attributed
to Da Vinci, and one of only two not held

00:12:53.540 --> 00:12:59.350
by a museum, this was sure to fetch an eye-popping
price.

00:12:59.350 --> 00:13:04.241
After a world tour of exhibitions, Leonardo’s
Salvator Mundi was sold by Christie’s New

00:13:04.241 --> 00:13:14.589
York for $450,312,500 to Saudi Crown Prince
Mohammed bin Salman—an all-time record price

00:13:14.589 --> 00:13:17.610
for the sale of a piece of art.

00:13:17.610 --> 00:13:23.029
Prior to the recognition by top museums that
this piece was, in fact, a Da Vinci, the value

00:13:23.029 --> 00:13:24.879
was in the low thousands.

00:13:24.879 --> 00:13:28.160
After, it was in the hundreds of millions.

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:33.620
That’s to say, art has no intrinsic value.

00:13:33.620 --> 00:13:38.430
The value for what Christie’s, Sotheby’s,
or other top auction houses sell is all in

00:13:38.430 --> 00:13:42.170
who painted it, who owned it, and who wants
it.

00:13:42.170 --> 00:13:47.350
An uninformed person can look at a car, or
a plate of food, or a house and guess with

00:13:47.350 --> 00:13:52.660
some accuracy what it’s worth, but that’s
not the case with art.

00:13:52.660 --> 00:13:57.460
Nearly all of the value is invisible, because
the value is simply what other people are

00:13:57.460 --> 00:13:59.110
willing to pay.

00:13:59.110 --> 00:14:04.959
Now, here’s the question: would you genuinely
believe that if you had bought that dilapidated

00:14:04.959 --> 00:14:09.210
portrait of Jesus at the estate auction in
New Orleans, you could have successfully convinced

00:14:09.210 --> 00:14:12.589
the National Gallery in London that it was
a true Da Vinci?

00:14:12.589 --> 00:14:17.949
Do you believe that if, back in 1960, Roy
Lichtenstein had met you, instead of Castelli,

00:14:17.949 --> 00:14:21.800
you could have successfully convinced enough
people of the artist’s future prospects

00:14:21.800 --> 00:14:24.779
to sell out his first show prior to opening?

00:14:24.779 --> 00:14:28.339
Any answer but no is arrogant.

00:14:28.339 --> 00:14:35.250
Value in the art world is defined by an incredibly
small number of gatekeepers—the curators,

00:14:35.250 --> 00:14:37.290
gallery owners, and auctioneers.

00:14:37.290 --> 00:14:43.810
So, to summarize, the art world is an incredibly
small, incredibly consolidated market where

00:14:43.810 --> 00:14:47.579
prices are almost entirely subjective.

00:14:47.579 --> 00:14:55.250
These three factors combined mean that, properly
executed, sellers can decide what prices are.

00:14:55.250 --> 00:15:01.610
Between 2005 and 2013, one fourth of all Andy
Warhol works put up up for auction were bought

00:15:01.610 --> 00:15:04.680
by a single man: Jose Mugrabi.

00:15:04.680 --> 00:15:10.399
Now, Mugrabi owns over 800 Warhol artworks
in total, representing close to a billion

00:15:10.399 --> 00:15:13.040
dollars in value, at least on paper.

00:15:13.040 --> 00:15:19.680
Therefore, Mugrabi is highly, highly invested
in making sure that the Warhol market stays

00:15:19.680 --> 00:15:20.680
hot.

00:15:20.680 --> 00:15:26.339
Now, there is, of course, a reason why in
more traditional markets, monopolies and oligopolies

00:15:26.339 --> 00:15:32.139
are disallowed: when power is too concentrated,
a single player can influence the entire market

00:15:32.139 --> 00:15:33.680
to their own benefit.

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:40.130
That’s exactly what Mugrabi does: whenever
a Warhol is up for sale at auction, he’ll

00:15:40.130 --> 00:15:41.130
be there.

00:15:41.130 --> 00:15:45.230
Often, Mugrabi will be one of the first to
bid, and will work to push up prices with

00:15:45.230 --> 00:15:46.779
his early offers.

00:15:46.779 --> 00:15:52.300
Mugrabi is more than willing to strategically
overpay for a Warhol work, because he knows

00:15:52.300 --> 00:15:53.610
that it’ll be worth it.

00:15:53.610 --> 00:15:59.420
That’s because auctions act as the only
publicly-available index of art prices.

00:15:59.420 --> 00:16:04.069
When one private individual sells artwork
to another private individual, the price is

00:16:04.069 --> 00:16:06.579
not public-knowledge.

00:16:06.579 --> 00:16:11.899
When one private individual sells artwork
at auction, the price is public-knowledge.

00:16:11.899 --> 00:16:17.680
So, that means that Mugrabi is willing to
go to an auction and bid copiously, regardless

00:16:17.680 --> 00:16:20.329
of actual market value.

00:16:20.329 --> 00:16:23.949
Either he’ll have pushed up the price for
another bidder, which is good for him, or

00:16:23.949 --> 00:16:27.290
he’ll have overpaid for the art, which is
still good for him.

00:16:27.290 --> 00:16:32.459
That’s because either way, the fact that
a Warhol work sold for a good price and the

00:16:32.459 --> 00:16:37.829
world knows it pushes up the hypothetical
value of the rest of his Warhol pieces, which

00:16:37.829 --> 00:16:40.449
he can then sell for more in private.

00:16:40.449 --> 00:16:46.339
What’s more, this manner of market manipulation
is entirely legal.

00:16:46.339 --> 00:16:49.250
Collectors will chose a niche, and then dominate
it.

00:16:49.250 --> 00:16:54.410
Plus, given the small size of the high-end
art market, Mugrabi almost certainly knows

00:16:54.410 --> 00:16:59.610
all the other top Warhol collectors, and collectively,
they realize that if one of them sells a piece

00:16:59.610 --> 00:17:04.980
publicly for a bad price, it’ll hurt all
of their portfolio values, so an informal

00:17:04.980 --> 00:17:11.449
social cabal arises that further influences
the market and artificially inflates values.

00:17:11.449 --> 00:17:15.640
However, this is only the tip of the iceberg.

00:17:15.640 --> 00:17:20.880
The same market conditions that enable Mugrabi’s
influence mean that collusion and corruption

00:17:20.880 --> 00:17:23.350
are confoundingly easy.

00:17:23.350 --> 00:17:27.860
There isn't one, blockbuster scam at the center
of the art market.

00:17:27.860 --> 00:17:33.510
Rather, it’s a market composed of scams.

00:17:33.510 --> 00:17:38.520
For example, counterintuitively, wealthy individuals
can turn a profit by donating art.

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:44.031
It’s rather simple: in the US, when one
donates artwork to a non-profit museum, they

00:17:44.031 --> 00:17:45.720
get a tax-write off.

00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:50.730
That’s to say, if someone donates a painting
worth $10 million, they don’t have to pay

00:17:50.730 --> 00:17:56.400
taxes on $10 million of income which, in theory,
would save them about $4 million.

00:17:56.400 --> 00:18:01.680
Of course, given the difficulties in determining
art’s worth, the IRS requires expensive

00:18:01.680 --> 00:18:05.429
artwork to be professionally appraised prior
to a write-off.

00:18:05.429 --> 00:18:09.590
Considering all the aforementioned market
conditions, though, it’s not tough to manipulate

00:18:09.590 --> 00:18:12.210
an appraisal to go one’s way.

00:18:12.210 --> 00:18:17.750
Of the hundreds of thousands of artworks donated
each year, the IRS audits only a couple hundred,

00:18:17.750 --> 00:18:20.730
but even those few paint a stark picture.

00:18:20.730 --> 00:18:26.470
In 2018 and 2019, about a third of audited
artwork was found to be over valued in its

00:18:26.470 --> 00:18:29.400
appraisal by an average of 38%.

00:18:29.400 --> 00:18:35.380
In fact, overall, only 42% of artwork was
found to have been appraised correctly, in

00:18:35.380 --> 00:18:39.460
part thanks to the competing pressure for
some to undervalue their work when its received

00:18:39.460 --> 00:18:44.279
through inheritance, in order to reduce the
estate taxes paid upon transfer of ownership.

00:18:44.279 --> 00:18:49.670
So, a rich person could buy a piece of art
for $4 million, let it appreciate over a few

00:18:49.670 --> 00:18:54.371
years, shop around for a favorable appraisal,
overstate its value, rely on the fact that

00:18:54.371 --> 00:18:59.640
the IRS only audits a tiny percentage of pieces,
donate the art $10 million, and they’ll

00:18:59.640 --> 00:19:01.470
have already broken even.

00:19:01.470 --> 00:19:06.419
It doesn’t take much for the percentage
a wealthy person will save in taxes to eclipse

00:19:06.419 --> 00:19:08.550
their original purchase price.

00:19:08.550 --> 00:19:12.980
As a cherry on top, of course, the presence
of an artist’s work in a museum is known

00:19:12.980 --> 00:19:17.310
to inflate the value of their other art, so
if a wealthy person owns a collection of a

00:19:17.310 --> 00:19:21.840
given artist’s work, as is common, donations
earn them further money through the effect

00:19:21.840 --> 00:19:24.429
it has on the rest of their collection.

00:19:24.429 --> 00:19:27.260
In aggregate, the situation is this: art is
a highly exploitable market.

00:19:27.260 --> 00:19:33.070
It was created by rich people, for rich people,
so it suits their needs.

00:19:33.070 --> 00:19:39.090
Whether its tax avoidance, money laundering,
or simple price-fixing, the art market enables

00:19:39.090 --> 00:19:40.100
all.

00:19:40.100 --> 00:19:43.730
Despite this, it’s almost entirely unregulated.

00:19:43.730 --> 00:19:48.561
There are no official records of who owns
what or how much they paid, so it’s nearly

00:19:48.561 --> 00:19:51.910
impossible to prove any malpractice in the
market.

00:19:51.910 --> 00:19:57.049
It’s a largely lawless market, except for
the simple, practical rule that you must be

00:19:57.049 --> 00:20:00.020
able to spend millions to enter it.

00:20:00.020 --> 00:20:02.130
So, who loses out?

00:20:02.130 --> 00:20:05.070
Well, for one, artists.

00:20:05.070 --> 00:20:10.529
Rich people use artwork like trading cards—coveting
a select few for arbitrary reasons—which

00:20:10.529 --> 00:20:15.050
excludes those who are unable to persuade
the gatekeepers of their value—those who

00:20:15.050 --> 00:20:17.929
don’t fit the galleries’ vision of a marketable
artist.

00:20:17.929 --> 00:20:22.370
Art is no longer an exercise of skill, it’s
one of branding.

00:20:22.370 --> 00:20:26.950
Warhol’s are like Supreme, but for the wealthiest
percent of a percent.

00:20:26.950 --> 00:20:32.920
Who also loses out is us: everyone who is
unable to access this exclusive market by

00:20:32.920 --> 00:20:37.030
sinking millions of dollars into a single,
volatile investment.

00:20:37.030 --> 00:20:42.309
We lose out because we can’t conduct the
same financial wizardry as those who walk

00:20:42.309 --> 00:20:44.000
through Sotheby’s doors.

00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:48.950
We can access the stock market, where tax
avoidance, money laundering, and price-fixing

00:20:48.950 --> 00:20:53.970
are heavily regulated and monitored, but not
this market, where these ruses go largely

00:20:53.970 --> 00:20:55.510
unnoticed.

00:20:55.510 --> 00:21:01.120
The reality is that any market can be exploited,
which is why markets are regulated.

00:21:01.120 --> 00:21:06.490
The difference here is that art is a unique
market where individual players have both

00:21:06.490 --> 00:21:13.630
the influence to manipulate the market, and
the influence to keep the market manipulatable.

00:21:13.630 --> 00:21:17.130
Everything you’ve heard so far is about
the largely legal malpractice the art market

00:21:17.130 --> 00:21:22.549
enables, but it also makes illegal actions
like money laundering and forgery strikingly

00:21:22.549 --> 00:21:23.549
easy.

